VNpc: bound the talent, item and inventory-slot counts, which size a resize before anything is read.

`VNpc::load` takes three of its five element counts straight off the file and
hands each one to `resize` before a single object is read:

	auto talent_count = static_cast<std::size_t>(r.read_int()); // numTalents
	this->talents.resize(talent_count);
	...
	auto item_count = static_cast<size_t>(r.read_int());        // itemCount
	this->items.resize(item_count);
	...
	auto inv_slot_count = static_cast<uint32_t>(r.read_int());  // numInvSlots
	this->slots.resize(inv_slot_count);

Same shape as the mesh element counts patch 0036 bounds and the waynet counts
0037 bounds, and the talent loop has the same reason it cannot fail on its own:
`ReadArchive::read_object` past the end of the entry stream logs "Expected
object, got entry" and returns **null** rather than throwing, and nothing
dereferences a talent -- so it runs to the file's own count over reads that give
nothing back.

Measured on the unpatched reader against `zenkit-node`'s `npc` fixture world
with the count rewritten in place to 0x0FFFFFFF:

	numTalents   268 million null talents, 4.3 GB, and the world reports LOADED after 6.8 s
	itemCount    2.1 GB, then the null-item guard fires, 0.8 s
	numInvSlots  2.1 GB, then a type mismatch on the slot's `used` flag, 0.7 s

The first is the dangerous case -- an *absurd* count is the harmless one,
because `resize` throws `bad_alloc` and the loop is never entered. The other two
are bounded by the same argument patch 0037 used for the waypoint count: they
already fail loudly, but only after gigabytes are committed.

**Two of the five counts are deliberately not bounded.** `numOverlays` and the
news `NumOfEntries` drive `push_back` loops over plain fields, and the first
read past the end of the entry stream is a type mismatch that throws in 66 ms --
loud, like `parse_vob_tree`'s `childs<N>`, so a guard there would be hardening a
reader that already fails correctly.

All three are bounded by the bytes actually left in the reader, with the same
`_bytes_remaining` helper as `Mesh.cc`, `BspTree.cc` and `WayNet.cc`. The
per-object floor is four bytes, under the smallest header any of the three
archive formats can write, because a bound must never refuse a world the engine
loads; a slot is two entries at the least, so two bytes.

Found by construction, not by luck, and not by the existing sweep either:
`zenkit-node/tools/fuzz-world.js --counts` can only reach the INTEGER entries
its fixture happens to carry, and `minimal.g2.zen` has no `oCNpc` at all. The
`npc` fixture variant was added for exactly this reason.

